      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Terraform for Kubernetes\nKubernetes is a system that runs\ncontainers on a group of machines,\ncalled a cluster. You describe\nobjects, such as a Deployment that\nruns copies of a container or a\nService that gives them one\naddress. The cluster then keeps\nthe real state equal to the\ndescription. On AWS, Amazon EKS\n(Elastic Kubernetes Service) runs\nthe cluster's control plane for\nyou. The [Terraform\nmap](../terraform.md) explains\nTerraform itself.\n\nTerraform reaches a cluster on EKS\nthrough four providers:\n\n- `hashicorp/aws` builds the\ncluster itself: the control plane,\nthe node groups, add-ons and\naccess rules.\n- `hashicorp/kubernetes` creates\nKubernetes objects inside the\ncluster, one typed resource per\nkind, plus `kubernetes_manifest`\nfor any kind ([provider docs](http\ns://registry.terraform.io/provider\ns/hashicorp/kubernetes/latest/docs\n)).\n- `hashicorp/helm` installs Helm\ncharts. A chart is a package of\nKubernetes objects with settings,\ncalled values; one installed chart\nis a release ([provider docs](http\ns://registry.terraform.io/provider\ns/hashicorp/helm/latest/docs)).\n- A community `kubectl` provider\napplies raw YAML files, as the\n`kubectl apply` command does.\n\nThe unit is one cluster. The last\nthree providers need the cluster's\naddress, its certificate and a\nshort-lived token. They get them\nfrom the cluster that the first\nprovider built, which creates the\ncentral problem of this setup\n([Two APIs, One Depends on the Oth\ner](#two-apis-one-depends-on-the-o\nther-two-apis-one-depends-on-the-o\nther)).\n\nFive more ideas explain most\nplans:\n\n- Three records describe each\nrelease: the Terraform state,\nHelm's own record, and the live\nobjects ([Three Records of Truth](\n#three-records-of-truth-three-reco\nrds-of-truth)).\n- Each field of a live object has\none owner: Terraform, Helm, a\ncontroller or EKS ([Every Field\nHas One Owner](#every-field-has-on\ne-owner-every-field-has-one-owner)\n).\n- Custom resource definitions\n(CRDs) add new kinds to a cluster,\nand they have their own lifecycle\n([CRDs Have Their Own Lifecycle](#\ncrds-have-their-own-lifecycle-crds\n-have-their-own-lifecycle)).\n- A finished apply does not mean a\nhealthy cluster ([Apply Complete\nIs Not Healthy](#apply-complete-is\n-not-healthy-apply-complete-is-not\n-healthy)).\n- Identity crosses between AWS and\nKubernetes in two directions ([Two\nIdentity Bridges](#two-identity-br\nidges-two-identity-bridges)).\n\nNow compare Snowflake. Snowflake\nruns a statement once, and the\nobject stays as created.\nKubernetes runs control loops that\nkeep changing objects towards\ntheir description, and some\ncontrollers add fields of their\nown. So in Kubernetes, more of\nwhat a plan shows depends on who\nelse acted after the last apply.\n\nThe map has four parts. Mental\nModels explains the ideas above.\nReading a Kubernetes Configuration\nsays what you will see and where\nto look first. Pitfalls lists the\nfailures that experienced users\nplan around. Good and Bad\nDecisions compares the main design\nchoices.",
      "fontSize": 18,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 22.5,
      "containerId": "node-7cb90b1b15c2",
      "originalText": "Terraform for Kubernetes\nKubernetes is a system that runs containers on a group of machines, called a cluster. You describe objects, such as a Deployment that runs copies of a container or a Service that gives them one address. The cluster then keeps the real state equal to the description. On AWS, Amazon EKS (Elastic Kubernetes Service) runs the cluster's control plane for you. The [Terraform map](../terraform.md) explains Terraform itself.\n\nTerraform reaches a cluster on EKS through four providers:\n\n- `hashicorp/aws` builds the cluster itself: the control plane, the node groups, add-ons and access rules.\n- `hashicorp/kubernetes` creates Kubernetes objects inside the cluster, one typed resource per kind, plus `kubernetes_manifest` for any kind ([provider docs](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/kubernetes/latest/docs)).\n- `hashicorp/helm` installs Helm charts. A chart is a package of Kubernetes objects with settings, called values; one installed chart is a release ([provider docs](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/helm/latest/docs)).\n- A community `kubectl` provider applies raw YAML files, as the `kubectl apply` command does.\n\nThe unit is one cluster. The last three providers need the cluster's address, its certificate and a short-lived token. They get them from the cluster that the first provider built, which creates the central problem of this setup ([Two APIs, One Depends on the Other](#two-apis-one-depends-on-the-other-two-apis-one-depends-on-the-other)).\n\nFive more ideas explain most plans:\n\n- Three records describe each release: the Terraform state, Helm's own record, and the live objects ([Three Records of Truth](#three-records-of-truth-three-records-of-truth)).\n- Each field of a live object has one owner: Terraform, Helm, a controller or EKS ([Every Field Has One Owner](#every-field-has-one-owner-every-field-has-one-owner)).\n- Custom resource definitions (CRDs) add new kinds to a cluster, and they have their own lifecycle ([CRDs Have Their Own Lifecycle](#crds-have-their-own-lifecycle-crds-have-their-own-lifecycle)).\n- A finished apply does not mean a healthy cluster ([Apply Complete Is Not Healthy](#apply-complete-is-not-healthy-apply-complete-is-not-healthy)).\n- Identity crosses between AWS and Kubernetes in two directions ([Two Identity Bridges](#two-identity-bridges-two-identity-bridges)).\n\nNow compare Snowflake. Snowflake runs a statement once, and the object stays as created. Kubernetes runs control loops that keep changing objects towards their description, and some controllers add fields of their own. So in Kubernetes, more of what a plan shows depends on who else acted after the last apply.\n\nThe map has four parts. Mental Models explains the ideas above. Reading a Kubernetes Configuration says what you will see and where to look first. Pitfalls lists the failures that experienced users plan around. Good and Bad Decisions compares the main design choices.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "terraform-kubernetes"
      }
    },
