      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Two APIs, One Depends on the Other\nTerraform builds the cluster through the AWS\nAPI. Then it talks to the Kubernetes API with\ncredentials that come from that same cluster.\nThe Kubernetes provider warns that the cluster\nand the Kubernetes resources should not be\ncreated in the same module. Its docs call\nseparate applies \"the most reliable way\"\n([provider docs](https://github.com/hashicorp/\nterraform-provider-kubernetes/blob/v3.5.0/docs\n/index.md#L42-L44)). Its EKS example therefore\nuses two folders: one for the cluster and one\nfor what runs inside ([EKS example](https://gi\nthub.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-kubernet\nes/blob/v3.5.0/_examples/eks/README.md)).\n\nAn EKS token is short-lived: AWS signs it for\n15 minutes, and the AWS command-line tool\nmarks it as expired after 14 ([get-token code]\n(https://github.com/aws/aws-cli/blob/2.42.1/aw\nscli/customizations/eks/get_token.py#L132-L136\n)). An `exec` block in the provider settings\nruns `aws eks get-token` for a fresh token on\nevery run ([exec plugins](https://github.com/h\nashicorp/terraform-provider-kubernetes/blob/v3\n.5.0/docs/index.md#L108-L124)). So the machine\nthat runs Terraform needs the AWS command-line\ntool.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-861eff220a0f",
      "originalText": "Two APIs, One Depends on the Other\nTerraform builds the cluster through the AWS API. Then it talks to the Kubernetes API with credentials that come from that same cluster. The Kubernetes provider warns that the cluster and the Kubernetes resources should not be created in the same module. Its docs call separate applies \"the most reliable way\" ([provider docs](https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-kubernetes/blob/v3.5.0/docs/index.md#L42-L44)). Its EKS example therefore uses two folders: one for the cluster and one for what runs inside ([EKS example](https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-kubernetes/blob/v3.5.0/_examples/eks/README.md)).\n\nAn EKS token is short-lived: AWS signs it for 15 minutes, and the AWS command-line tool marks it as expired after 14 ([get-token code](https://github.com/aws/aws-cli/blob/2.42.1/awscli/customizations/eks/get_token.py#L132-L136)). An `exec` block in the provider settings runs `aws eks get-token` for a fresh token on every run ([exec plugins](https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-kubernetes/blob/v3.5.0/docs/index.md#L108-L124)). So the machine that runs Terraform needs the AWS command-line tool.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "two-apis-one-depends-on-the-other"
      }
    },
