    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 2125860982,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-399370e8c63e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1288.8,
      "y": 1420.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 350.0,
      "seed": 726129,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Two Identity Bridges\nIdentity crosses between AWS and Kubernetes in\ntwo directions:\n\n- From AWS into Kubernetes: an IAM principal\nbecomes a Kubernetes user. EKS does this with\naccess entries, managed through the AWS API,\nor with the older `aws-auth` ConfigMap inside\nthe cluster ([access to clusters](https://docs\n.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/grant-k8s\n-access.html)).\n- From a pod out to AWS: a pod gets an IAM\nrole. EKS does this with IAM roles for service\naccounts (IRSA), or with EKS Pod Identity\n([pod identities](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/\neks/latest/userguide/pod-identities.html)).\n\nAccess entries are managed only through the\nAWS provider, so granting access needs no\nworking connection to the cluster.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-399370e8c63e",
      "originalText": "Two Identity Bridges\nIdentity crosses between AWS and Kubernetes in two directions:\n\n- From AWS into Kubernetes: an IAM principal becomes a Kubernetes user. EKS does this with access entries, managed through the AWS API, or with the older `aws-auth` ConfigMap inside the cluster ([access to clusters](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/grant-k8s-access.html)).\n- From a pod out to AWS: a pod gets an IAM role. EKS does this with IAM roles for service accounts (IRSA), or with EKS Pod Identity ([pod identities](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/pod-identities.html)).\n\nAccess entries are managed only through the AWS provider, so granting access needs no working connection to the cluster.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "two-identity-bridges"
      }
    },
