    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 1090618160,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-fe71c58c37f1",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1321.2,
      "y": 1940.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 630.0,
      "seed": 794403,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Where to Look First\nRead a Kubernetes configuration in this order:\n\n1. `required_providers`, the lock file and the\nTerraform version pin. The helm provider\nchanged its syntax in version 3, so the\nversion tells you which docs apply ([v3\nupgrade guide](https://github.com/hashicorp/te\nrraform-provider-helm/blob/v3.3.0/docs/guides/\nv3-upgrade-guide.md)).\n2. The `provider \"kubernetes\"` and `provider\n\"helm\"` blocks. Find where the host,\ncertificate and token come from. If the\ncluster is built in the same configuration,\nlook for a documented bootstrap order.\n3. The backend and how environments switch:\nworkspaces, variable files or backend\nsettings.\n4. The pipeline: plan and apply, saved plan or\nnot, approvals, `-target` steps and secret\ninjection.\n5. The cluster definition: version, access\nmode, access entries, add-on pins, endpoint\naccess and node groups.\n6. Each `helm_release`: the version pin,\n`wait`, `atomic`, `timeout`, CRD handling and\nsecrets in values.\n7. Each custom resource: which CRD it needs,\nwhich chart installs that CRD, and which\nresource type applies it.\n8. The secret paths: `kubernetes_secret`, Helm\nvalues, and an operator that reads a secret\nstore.\n9. The `lifecycle` blocks, `ignore_changes`,\nand checksum annotations. Each one marks a\nproblem someone already met.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-fe71c58c37f1",
      "originalText": "Where to Look First\nRead a Kubernetes configuration in this order:\n\n1. `required_providers`, the lock file and the Terraform version pin. The helm provider changed its syntax in version 3, so the version tells you which docs apply ([v3 upgrade guide](https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-helm/blob/v3.3.0/docs/guides/v3-upgrade-guide.md)).\n2. The `provider \"kubernetes\"` and `provider \"helm\"` blocks. Find where the host, certificate and token come from. If the cluster is built in the same configuration, look for a documented bootstrap order.\n3. The backend and how environments switch: workspaces, variable files or backend settings.\n4. The pipeline: plan and apply, saved plan or not, approvals, `-target` steps and secret injection.\n5. The cluster definition: version, access mode, access entries, add-on pins, endpoint access and node groups.\n6. Each `helm_release`: the version pin, `wait`, `atomic`, `timeout`, CRD handling and secrets in values.\n7. Each custom resource: which CRD it needs, which chart installs that CRD, and which resource type applies it.\n8. The secret paths: `kubernetes_secret`, Helm values, and an operator that reads a secret store.\n9. The `lifecycle` blocks, `ignore_changes`, and checksum annotations. Each one marks a problem someone already met.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "k8s-where-to-look-first"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
